---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatXml from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatXml.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="xml-formatter">
  <DevFormatXml client:load mode="format" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the formatter decides where to break</h2>
    <p>
      Mixed content makes XML indentation difficult. The XML formatter walks the parsed document and applies
      four rules:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>An element whose children are all elements gets each child on its own line, indented one level.</li>
      <li>An element with a single short text child stays on one line: <code>&lt;price&gt;44.95&lt;/price&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li>An element with mixed content (text and elements together) stays exactly as written, because the whitespace between those children is part of the rendered text.</li>
      <li>An element with more attributes than fit on one line gets each attribute on its own line, which makes a WSDL or an Android layout readable.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Rule three keeps the output correct. If <code>&lt;p&gt;An &lt;em&gt;important&lt;/em&gt; note&lt;/p&gt;</code>
      is spread across three lines, whitespace appears on both sides of the emphasis and the reader sees a
      different text. The tool detects this case, leaves it unchanged, and tells you.
    </p>

    <h2>Working with SOAP and WSDL</h2>
    <p>
      SOAP envelopes arrive as one very long line, so they need formatting. Two points:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A fragment copied from a larger envelope usually fails to parse, because the namespace prefixes
        (<code>soap:</code>, <code>xsi:</code>, <code>wsse:</code>) are declared on the root element you did
        not copy. Copy from <code>&lt;soap:Envelope&gt;</code> down, or add the <code>xmlns:</code>
        declarations to the fragment.
      </li>
      <li>
        WSDL documents have many attributes, so the attribute-wrapping rule applies often. A
        <code>&lt;wsdl:operation&gt;</code> with six attributes on one 200-character line is unreadable.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the XML formatter keeps</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Construct</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>&lt;?xml … ?&gt;</code> declaration</td><td>Kept at the top, verbatim</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE …&gt;</code></td><td>Kept, including an internal subset</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Comments</td><td>Kept by default, one per line; removable with a switch</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CDATA sections</td><td>Reproduced byte-for-byte, contents untouched</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Processing instructions</td><td>Kept on their own line</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Attribute order</td><td>Unchanged; order has no meaning in XML, but it matters in diffs</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Empty elements</td><td>Collapsed to <code>&lt;a/&gt;</code> by default; switchable</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Entity references</td><td>Resolved by the parser and re-escaped on output</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The parser is your browser&rsquo;s</h2>
    <p>
      This page has no bundled XML library. It parses with <code>DOMParser</code>, the same implementation the
      browser uses for XHR responses, XSLT and <code>fetch</code>. So a document that is well-formed here is
      well-formed in a real consumer. The error messages come from the browser engine, so the tool turns them
      into one plain sentence and keeps the raw text behind a disclosure.
    </p>

    <h2>Formatting private XML</h2>
    <p>
      Enterprise XML often contains sensitive data: SOAP headers with WS-Security tokens, HL7 and FHIR patient
      records, financial messages, SAML assertions with a whole identity. The processing happens in this tab.
      Expand the panel under the tool to watch the request counter, or turn off your network and check that
      the page still works.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
